chx:F1 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Background"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Chapter 2 of the 3rd edition introduces the Boolean values True and False, the six comparison operators, the Boolean operators and, or and not, how comparison and Boolean operators mix, the components of flow control - conditions and blocks - and the if, else and elif statements, with two short programs." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01 .
chx:F2 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Python 3.10 added structural pattern matching: a match statement compares a value against successive case patterns, closer to pattern matching than to the switch statement of C or Java. Python 3.14 adopted PEP 765, which disallows return, break and continue that exit a finally block." ; sen0414:cites chx:P06, chx:P02, chx:P08 .
chx:F3 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The reference documentation makes precise what the chapter leaves informal: and and or return one of their operands rather than a Boolean - x and y returns x if x is false - and evaluate the second operand only when needed; comparisons chain, so x < y <= z means x < y and y <= z; many values besides False count as false, including None, zero of any numeric type and empty sequences; the conditional expression x if C else y chooses a value in one line; and PEP 8 advises against comparing Boolean values to True or False with ==." ; sen0414:cites chx:P04, chx:P05, chx:P10 .
chx:F4 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Conclusion"@en ; sen0414:findingText "For an advanced course, chapter 2 is best taught as decision-making in real Python: truthiness and short-circuit evaluation as working tools, chained comparisons and conditional expressions as idioms, and the match statement as the modern alternative to long elif chains." ; sen0414:cites chx:P04, chx:P05, chx:P06 .
chx:F5 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Terms used without definition"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter text named ideas that a learner could not look up in the corpus itself: the None value, containers and their emptiness, identity and the is operator, the order of strings, the arity of an operator, truth tables, operands and the result of and and or, precedence among not, and and or, keywords and soft keywords, indentation and the clause, the assignment expression, the wildcard, sequence and guard patterns, singletons, flowcharts, the exception and the traceback; and this record's own inventory listed block, the Boolean operators and the flow control statement (C_12, C_11, C_13) without the domain ontology carrying their parts. The Python glossary was read for the statement, the suite, the sequence and the singleton; the lexical analysis reference for indentation, tokens and keywords; the data model for identity and None; the built-in constants and functions for None, True, False and ord; the exceptions documentation and the tutorial chapter on errors for the exception, the traceback and the error kinds; the sys documentation for sys.exit; the keyword module for hard and soft keywords; the simple statements reference for pass; What's New in Python 3.10 for the match statement and PEP 572 for the assignment expression. Each concept section of document 1.1.0 is written from those texts, and each of its code claims is executed under CPython 3.14.4 and 3.12.11." ; sen0414:cites chx:P11, chx:P12, chx:P13, chx:P14, chx:P15, chx:P16, chx:P17, chx:P18, chx:P19, chx:P20, chx:P21, chx:P22 .
chx:Scorecard a sen0414:QualityScorecard ; rdfs:label "Quality scorecard"@en ; sen0414:topicCoverage "25 primary-source concepts inventoried from the textbook ontology; the domain ontology of 1.1.0 carries 73 concepts, 41 of them added so that every term the text uses is explained." ; sen0414:citationStrength "22 sources: P01 to P10 opened and verified at 2026-09-24T21:19:31 and read again on 2026-10-01, P11 to P22 opened and read on 2026-10-01; 19 cited by findings; the rest (P03, P07, P09) consulted and cited by the corpus text." ; sen0414:methodologyCompliance "RDODI procedure v1.6.0 Stage 1." ; sen0414:reproducibility "Every source is a stable public URL; every claim names its sources." .
